om the wheelbase", "seek": 0, "fit": true, "spot": ["calib", "canvas"], "text": "The yellow line joins the two wheel centres. A 2006 Ridgeline's wheelbase is <b>3.10 m</b>, which gives the scale (139 px = 1 m). (0, 0) is the rear wheel centre; <b>x</b> points forward (toward the barrier), <b>y</b> up."}, {"title": "Check: impact speed", "seek": 8, "select": "Placard", "plot": {"q": "vx", "show": ["Placard"]}, "spot": ["plots", "readout"], "text": "Forward speed of the body. Before it hits, the app measures <b>15.7 m/s = 56.4 km/h</b> \u2014 the test is run at 56.3 km/h, so the tracking, the scale and the timing all check out. Then it drops to zero in about 70 ms and goes slightly negative: the truck <b>bounces back</b> at about 1.5 m/s."}, {"title": "Deceleration in g", "seek": 36, "select": "Placard", "plot": {"q": "ax", "show": ["Placard"]}, "spot": ["plots", "time"], "text": "Acceleration along x in m/s\u00b2 (\u00f7 9.81 = g). The truck goes from 15.7 m/s to a stop in <b>85 ms</b> \u2014 an <b>average of 19 g</b>; that number is solid. The peak here is about <b>30 g</b> at 56 ms, but peaks are fragile: acceleration comes from differentiating position twice, so it depends on <i>Smoothing</i> (set to 30 Hz here; try 15 or 60 in <i>Time &amp; filtering</i> \u2014 the peak moves between ~25 and ~45 g). Crash labs use accelerometers for exact peaks."}, {"title": "Crush distance", "seek": 46, "select": "Placard", "plot": {"q": "x", "show": ["Placard"]}, "spot": ["plots", "canvas"], "text": "Position of the body. After touching the barrier it keeps going another <b>0.74 m</b> \u2014 that's how far the front end crushed. The trail shows the path; this frame is maximum crush."}, {"title": "The front wheel is pushed back", "seek": 46, "plot": {"q": "distance", "show": ["Wheelbase"]}, "spot": ["measures", "plots"], "text": "<b>Wheelbase</b> = distance between the two wheel centres. It starts at 3.10 m and shrinks to <b>2.87 m</b>: the crash shoves the front wheel ~23 cm backwards. <i>Pitch</i> (hidden; tick it) shows the nose diving."}, {"title": "Whole wheels, not spokes", "seek": 20, "zoom": [799.7952931750649, 422.374035849258, 3.0], "select": "Front wheel", "spot": ["points", "canvas"], "text": "The wheels spin fast, so a small patch on the spokes would lose track. The wheel points use a big template (solid box) covering the whole wheel \u2014 its round outline doesn't change as it spins. Set this per point with <i>Template</i> in the point's settings."}, {"title": "Get the numbers out", "spot": ["act:export"], "text": "<b>Export CSV</b> (Ctrl+E) writes time, positions, velocities and accelerations for every frame. <b>Export video</b> renders this with the overlays."}], "stab": {}}